kokkos-stdexec: 3d LJ melt. On CUDA the force zero runs on its own stream
and overlaps the halo exchange. The pair kernel waits for that zero on
the device, not through a host fence. The remaining host fence is the one
before reverse communication.

Build LAMMPS with the KOKKOS package and an external kokkos-stdexec:

  cmake -S cmake -B build-stdexec \
    -C cmake/presets/kokkos-stdexec.cmake \
    -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -D CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ -D CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD=20 \
    -D EXTERNAL_KOKKOS_STDEXEC=ON \
    -D Kokkos_ROOT=/path/to/kokkos/install \
    -D KokkosStdexec_ROOT=/path/to/kokkos-stdexec/install \
    -D stdexec_ROOT=/path/to/stdexec/install

The preset already sets PKG_KOKKOS=ON and EXTERNAL_KOKKOS_STDEXEC=ON (which
implies EXTERNAL_KOKKOS). The original variant for comparison is the same
configure without the preset and without EXTERNAL_KOKKOS_STDEXEC,
KokkosStdexec_ROOT and stdexec_ROOT; add -D PKG_KOKKOS=ON -D EXTERNAL_KOKKOS=ON.

The setup banner should include:
  Kokkos stdexec: force zero overlaps comm; pair waits on device

Run:

  lmp -k on g 1 -sf kk -in in.lj
  mpirun -np 4 lmp -k on g 4 -sf kk -in in.lj

Benchmark original vs kokkos-stdexec (builds both, runs 4 ranks x 1 GPU):

  bench/bench_stdexec.sh all

See the header of bench/bench_stdexec.sh for options.
